sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: To find the greatest common measure of two given commensurable magnitudes.
----
proof:

Let the two given commensurable magnitudes be {AB} and {CD} with {AB} the less.

It is required to find the greatest common measure of {AB} and {CD}.

Now the magnitude {AB} either measures {CD} or it does not.

If it measures it, and it does measures itself, then {AB} is a common measure of {AB} and {CD}. And it is manifest that it is also the greatest, for a greater magnitude than the magnitude {AB} does not measure {AB}.

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="300" height="100" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="X.3 diagram" src="propX3.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    // Joyce's figure draws AB, CD and G. The proof works with the pieces the
    // subtraction leaves — ED, EC on CD and FB, AF on AB — so those are added
    // as transparent highlight targets (;0;0;0); the resting figure is
    // unchanged. The walk is highlight beats only: each "measures" step
    // lights the two magnitudes it compares.
    //
    // Draggables (checked against the bundle): A, B, C, D (the givens,
    // standing from slide 1), G1, G2 (G's ends), and the hidden far points
    // A0/A3/C0/C3. G enters only in the reductio, so G1/G2 are deferred and
    // withdrawn again once the contradiction is reached. E and F are cutoff
    // points, listed per slide.
    var GIVEN = ["A","B","AB","C","D","CD"];
    var SUB   = GIVEN.concat(["E","ED","EC"]);
    var REM   = SUB.concat(["F","FB","AF"]);
    var HYP   = REM.concat(["G","G1","G2"]);
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_0",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "X.3",
        elements: [
            "A0;point;free;-1000,35;0;0",
            "A3;point;free;1000,35;0;0",
            "C0;point;free;-1000,70;0;0",
            "C3;point;free;1000,70;0;0",
            "A;point;lineSlider;A0,A3,160,0",
            "B;point;lineSlider;A0,A3,260,0",
            "AB;line;connect;A,B",
            "C;point;lineSlider;C0,C3,30,0",
            "D;point;lineSlider;C0,C3,260,0",
            "CD;line;connect;C,D",
            "E1;point;cutoff;D,C,A,B;0;0",
            "E;point;cutoff;E1,C,A,B",
            "F1;point;cutoff;B,A,C,E;0;0",
            "F;point;cutoff;F1,A,C,E",
            "G1;point;lineSlider;A0,A3,30,0;0",
            "G2;point;lineSlider;A0,A3,80,0;0",
            "G;line;connect;G1,G2;black",
            // Highlight targets only — AB and CD already draw these.
            "ED;line;connect;E,D;0;0;0",
            "EC;line;connect;E,C;0;0;0",
            "FB;line;connect;F,B;0;0;0",
            "AF;line;connect;A,F;0;0;0"
        ],
        deferDraggables: ["G1","G2"],
        aliases: { "DE": "ED", "CE": "EC", "BF": "FB", "FA": "AF",
                   "BA": "AB", "DC": "CD" },
        slides: [
            { text: "“To find the greatest common measure of two given commensurable magnitudes.”",
              visible: GIVEN,
              highlighted: ["AB","CD"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "X.3" }] },
            { text: "Let the two given commensurable magnitudes be {AB} and {CD} with {AB} the less.",
              visible: GIVEN,
              highlighted: ["AB","CD"] },
            { text: "It is required to find the greatest common measure of {AB} and {CD}.",
              visible: GIVEN,
              highlighted: ["AB","CD"] },
            { text: "Now the magnitude {AB} either measures {CD} or it does not.",
              visible: GIVEN,
              highlighted: ["AB","CD"] },
            { text: "If it measures it, and it does measures itself, then {AB} is a common measure of {AB} and {CD}.",
              visible: GIVEN,
              highlighted: ["AB","CD"] },
            { text: "And it is manifest that it is also the greatest,",
              visible: GIVEN,
              highlighted: ["AB"] },
            { text: "for a greater magnitude than the magnitude {AB} does not measure {AB}.",
              visible: GIVEN,
              highlighted: ["AB"] },
            { text: "Next, let {AB} not measure {CD}.",
              visible: GIVEN,
              highlighted: ["AB","CD"] },
            { text: "Then, if the less is continually subtracted in turn from the greater,",
              visible: GIVEN,
              highlighted: ["AB","CD"] },
            { text: "then that which is left over will sometime measure the one before it, because {AB} and {CD} are not incommensurable.",
              visible: GIVEN,
              highlighted: ["AB","CD"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "the remainder will sometime measure the one before it", ref: "X.2" }] },
            { text: "Let {AB}, measuring {ED}, leave {EC} less than itself,",
              visible: SUB,
              highlighted: ["AB","ED","EC","E"],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "E", name: geomlib.A.Point.appear } ] } },
            { text: "let {EC}, measuring {FB}, leave {AF} less than itself, and let {AF} measure {CE|EC}.",
              visible: REM,
              highlighted: ["EC","FB","AF","F"],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "F", name: geomlib.A.Point.appear } ] } },
            { text: "Since, then, {AF} measures {CE|EC}, while {CE|EC} measures {FB}, therefore {AF} also measures {FB}.",
              visible: REM,
              highlighted: ["AF","EC","FB"] },
            { text: "But it measures itself also, therefore {AF} also measures the whole {AB}.",
              visible: REM,
              highlighted: ["AF","AB"] },
            { text: "But {AB} measures {DE|ED}, therefore {AF} also measures {ED}.",
              visible: REM,
              highlighted: ["AB","ED","AF"] },
            { text: "But it measures {CE|EC} also, therefore it also measures the whole {CD}.",
              visible: REM,
              highlighted: ["AF","EC","CD"] },
            { text: "Therefore {AF} is a common measure of {AB} and {CD}.",
              visible: REM,
              highlighted: ["AF","AB","CD"] },
            { text: "I say next that it is also the greatest.",
              visible: REM,
              highlighted: ["AF"] },
            { text: "If not, then there there is some magnitude {G} greater than {AF} which measures {AB} and {CD}.",
              visible: HYP,
              highlighted: ["G","AF","AB","CD"],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "G", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect } ] } },
            { text: "Since then {G} measures {AB}, while {AB} measures {ED}, therefore {G} also measures {ED}.",
              visible: HYP,
              highlighted: ["G","AB","ED"] },
            { text: "But it measures the whole {CD} also, therefore {G} measures the remainder {CE|EC}.",
              visible: HYP,
              highlighted: ["G","CD","EC"] },
            { text: "But {CE|EC} measures {FB}, therefore {G} also measures {FB}.",
              visible: HYP,
              highlighted: ["G","EC","FB"] },
            { text: "But it measures the whole {AB} also, and it therefore measures the remainder {AF},",
              visible: HYP,
              highlighted: ["G","AB","AF"] },
            { text: "the greater the less, which is impossible.",
              visible: HYP,
              highlighted: ["G","AF"] },
            // The hypothesis is withdrawn: G leaves the figure.
            { text: "Therefore no magnitude greater than {AF} measures {AB} and {CD}.",
              visible: REM,
              highlighted: ["AF","AB","CD"] },
            { text: "Therefore {AF} is the greatest common measure of {AB} and {CD}.",
              visible: REM,
              highlighted: ["AF","AB","CD"] },
            { text: "Therefore the greatest common measure of the two given commensurable magnitudes {AB} and {CD} has been found.",
              visible: REM,
              highlighted: ["AF","AB","CD"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "Q.E.D." }] }
        ],
        resolveJustification: function(ref) {
            if (ref === "X.3") return "#prop";
            return (window.eucrefs && window.eucrefs[ref]) || null;
        }
    });

    // Per-page citation lookup for the slideshow's resolveJustification.
    window.eucrefs = (window.eucrefs || {});
    Object.assign(window.eucrefs, {
        "X.2": "/elements/books/bookX/propositions/propX2/"
    });
</script>
</figure>

Next, let {AB} not measure {CD}.

Then, if the less is continually subtracted in turn from the greater, then that which is left over will sometime measure the one before it, because {AB} and {CD} are not incommensurable. [!just X.2]

Let {AB}, measuring {ED}, leave {EC} less than itself, let {EC}, measuring {FB}, leave {AF} less than itself, and let {AF} measure {CE}.

Since, then, {AF} measures {CE}, while {CE} measures {FB}, therefore {AF} also measures {FB}. But it measures itself also, therefore {AF} also measures the whole {AB}. But {AB} measures {DE}, therefore {AF} also measures {ED}. But it measures {CE} also, therefore it also measures the whole {CD}.

Therefore {AF} is a common measure of {AB} and {CD}.

I say next that it is also the greatest.

If not, then there there is some magnitude {G} greater than {AF} which measures {AB} and {CD}. Since then {G} measures {AB}, while {AB} measures {ED}, therefore {G} also measures {ED}.

But it measures the whole {CD} also, therefore {G} measures the remainder {CE}. But {CE} measures {FB}, therefore {G} also measures {FB}.

But it measures the whole {AB} also, and it therefore measures the remainder {AF}, the greater the less, which is impossible.

Therefore no magnitude greater than {AF} measures {AB} and {CD}. Therefore {AF} is the greatest common measure of {AB} and {CD}.

Therefore the greatest common measure of the two given commensurable magnitudes {AB} and {CD} has been found.

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
